## Why This Is Asked

This question probes a candidate's ability to validate edge logic at scale while balancing correctness, privacy, and performance—critical skills for managing distributed systems across multiple regions.

## Key Concepts

- Edge testing and shadow traffic for real-world validation
- Canary rollouts and feature flags for safe deployment
- Privacy and data isolation with synthetic profiles
- Drift detection and regression testing
- Rollback criteria and incident response automation

## Code Example

```javascript
function compareEdgeToOracle(edgeOutput, oracleOutput) {
  return {
    isMatch: JSON.stringify(edgeOutput) === JSON.stringify(oracleOutput),
    differences: diff(edgeOutput, oracleOutput)
  };
}

function evaluateCanaryMetrics(metrics) {
  const { errorRate, latencyP99, driftScore } = metrics;
  return {
    shouldRollback: errorRate > 0.01 || latencyP99 > 100 || driftScore > 0.05,
    status: errorRate > 0.01 ? 'ERROR' : latencyP99 > 100 ? 'DEGRADED' : 'HEALTHY'
  };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Real-time collaboration applications face significant timing and consistency challenges across distributed services. This question evaluates your ability to design practical testing strategies that verify message ordering, presence accuracy, and read receipt functionality under realistic load conditions and network failures, with emphasis on concrete data validation and automated integration testing.

## Key Concepts

- End-to-end message delivery guarantees and in-order semantics
- Presence tracking accuracy during network partitions and recovery
- Cross-shard consistency for read receipts and typing indicators
- Data-driven testing approaches with realistic user behavior patterns
- Automated validation integrated into CI/CD pipelines

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-test harness sketch for in-order delivery verification
let lastSeq = {};
const validateOrder = (roomId, message) => {
  if (lastSeq[roomId] >= message.sequenceId) {
    throw new Error(`Out-of-order message in room ${roomId}`);
  }
  lastSeq[roomId] = message.sequenceId;
};
```

## Why This Is Asked

This question probes practical end-to-end testing of a distributed MongoDB search feature, focusing on ranking accuracy, fuzziness robustness, pagination, performance KPIs, and resilience under failover and repartitioning. It also emphasizes realistic CI integration and data generation strategies.

## Key Concepts

- End-to-end search tests for ranking and fuzziness
- Performance metrics: p95 latency, throughput, error rate
- Resilience: replica failover, shard migration, partitions
- Data modeling: synthetic boosts and realistic term distributions
- CI data management and flaky-test handling

## Code Example

```javascript
// Example test sketch (pseudo)
async function testSearchRanking() {
  const res = await api.search('coffee', { fuzziness: 1, page: 1, perPage: 10 })
  if (res.results.length !== 10) throw new Error('unexpected length')
  if (res.results[0].score < res.results[1].score) throw new Error('ranking not sorted')
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you validate ranking under load spikes and index churn?
- How would you detect and isolate flaky tests caused by replication lag?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of practical testing strategy, not just theory. Meta wants engineers who can balance speed and confidence in testing.

## Expected Answer
Strong candidates mention: unit tests for individual components/utils, integration tests for component interactions, E2E for user workflows. They should discuss coverage trade-offs and mutation testing for quality assurance.

## Code Example
```typescript
// Unit test example
describe('Button', () => {
  it('renders with correct text', () => {
    render(<Button>Click me</Button>)
    expect(screen.getByRole('button')).toHaveTextContent('Click me')
  })
})

// Integration test example
describe('Form submission', () => {
  it('submits form with valid data', async () => {
    const { getByRole, getByLabelText } = render(<ContactForm />)
    fireEvent.change(getByLabelText('Email'), { target: { value: 'test@example.com' } })
    fireEvent.click(getByRole('button', { name: 'Submit' }))
    await waitFor(() => expect(mockSubmit).toHaveBeenCalledWith({ email: 'test@example.com' }))
  })
})
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle testing third-party integrations?
- When would you use mutation testing vs traditional coverage?
- How do you determine which E2E tests are worth maintaining?

