## Why This Is Asked
This question probes testing of a real-time, multi-tenant streaming system with shared infrastructure (Kafka, MongoDB). It tests end-to-end latency, exactly-once processing, tenant isolation, privacy-focused test data, and canary-based deployment practices.

## Key Concepts
- End-to-end latency (p95/p99)
- Exactly-once semantics and deduping
- Tenant isolation on shared resources
- Data privacy and redaction in tests
- Canary deployments and rollback criteria

## Code Example
```javascript
// Dedup logic in consumer
let seen = new Set();
while ((ev = nextEvent()) !== null) {
  if (seen.has(ev.id)) continue;
  seen.add(ev.id);
  process(ev);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you measure latency under spike and set alert thresholds?
- How would you rollback a failing canary without affecting tenants?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests privacy-sensitive data sharing across tenants, with explicit consent, retention policies, and data residency constraints. This assesses end-to-end testing, data lineage, masking, and the ability to fail closed (rollback) on privacy signals.

## Key Concepts
- Privacy-preserving data sharing
- Consent management and per-tenant gating
- End-to-end and contract testing across API, event bus, and warehouse
- Data lineage, masking, retention and residency rules
- Drift detection and rollback/kill-switch criteria

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-test sketch: verify per-tenant consent gating before data is emitted
test('consent_gate prevents emission when not given', () => {
  const tenant = {id:'t1', consent:true};
  const data = extractMetrics(tenant);
  expect(emitAllowed(tenant, data)).toBe(true);
  tenant.consent = false;
  expect(emitAllowed(tenant, data)).toBe(false);
});
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test data-residency constraints across regions with a multi-region warehouse?
- How would you simulate a privacy violation and verify rollback gates and audit logs?

## Why This Is Asked
Real-time pipelines present subtle consistency and resilience challenges that unit tests alone cannot catch. Interviewers want to see that you understand end-to-end testing across distributed systems, including CI/CD integration.

## Key Concepts
- Exactly-once semantics via transactional sinks and checkpointing
- Stateful stream processing and its failure modes
- Backward/forward schema evolution to prevent consumer breakage
- Backpressure handling, failure recovery, and replay safety
- Observability within CI/CD pipelines

## Code Example
```javascript
// Verify exactly-once processing with idempotent writes
const result = await warehouse.query(`
  SELECT event_id, COUNT(*) as score_count
  FROM risk_scores
  GROUP BY event_id
  HAVING COUNT(*) > 1
`);
assert(result.length === 0, 'Duplicate events detected');

// Validate replay safety after simulated outage
await kafka.broker.crash({ node: 3 });
await flink.restartFromCheckpoint();
const postRecoveryScores = await warehouse.fetchLatestScores();
assert(postRecoveryScores.length === expectedCount);
```

## Why This Is Asked

Helps assess ability to design practical, scalable data pipeline tests across components and ensure data fidelity under real-world pressures.

## Key Concepts

- End-to-end testing for streaming pipelines
- Idempotency and exactly-once processing
- Schema evolution and compatibility testing
- Backpressure, replay, and volume ramp strategies

## Code Example

```javascript
// Simple synthetic event generator with schema version
function generateEvent(version, seq) {
  const base = { id: `evt-${seq}`, ts: Date.now(), version };
  if (version === 2) base.meta = { source: 'sensor-A' };
  return JSON.stringify(base);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you validate idempotent upserts if a retry occurs mid-stream?
- How would you test schema evolution across multiple topics and downstream services?
- Which metrics and dashboards help verify data quality during backpressure?

## Why This Is Asked
Examines end-to-end streaming test strategy, including schema evolution, hot-swapping workers, and fault tolerance under real-world load.

## Key Concepts
- End-to-end streaming tests with at-least-once semantics
- Schema evolution compatibility and registry integration
- Canary deployments, fault tolerance, and ordering guarantees
- Idempotent sinks and replayable test data

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo test scaffold for streaming end-to-end
const testEventsV1 = generateEvents(1000, {version:1});
produceToKafka(testEventsV1);
updateSchemaRegistry({version:2});
const results = consumeFromSink();
assertOrdering(results);
assertDuplicates(results) < 0.001;
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you quantify the impact of duplicates on downstream analytics?
- What automated rollback criteria would you implement for canaries?
